---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/particle-size-distribution-number-vs-volume';
const title = 'Particle Size Distribution in Cleaning Trials: Number vs Volume';
const seoTitle = 'Particle Size Distribution: Number vs Volume';
const description = 'Review particle size reports for a cleaning trial with an original sphere example, a comparison table and questions to clarify the reporting basis.';
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-11-07', dateModified: '2026-11-07', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Particle Size Distribution</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Surface Cleaning</span><span class="blog-tag">Particle Size</span><span class="blog-tag">Trial Reports</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">A cleaning-trial discussion includes two summaries of the same particle population: one says the small group is about 89%, the other says 50%. Before treating this as a disagreement, ask what each percentage measures. Our hypothetical example shows the question to resolve.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>November 7, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>3 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/particle-size-number-volume-v1.jpg" alt="Hypothetical spheres: eight with diameter d and one with diameter 2d, compared by number and volume." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original arithmetic illustration of one unchanged population. These percentages are composition fractions, not cleaning-removal results.</em></p>

      <section id="distribution-basis">
        <h2>Identify what the distribution weighs</h2>
        <p>The public terminology in <a href="https://www.iso.org/obp/ui?_escaped_fragment_=iso%3Astd%3Aiso%3A19430%3Aed-2%3Av1%3Aen" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 19430:2024, clause 3.5</a> describes particle size distribution in terms of particle quantity across sizes. The quantity may be based on number, mass or volume. Here we compare number and volume only.</p>
        <p>ISO 19430 concerns particle tracking analysis in liquid dispersions. We use its terminology; it is not a dry-film cleaning acceptance standard.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="sphere-example">
        <h2>Work through one nine-particle population</h2>
        <p>Imagine eight spheres with diameter <em>d</em> and one sphere with diameter 2<em>d</em>. Let <em>V</em> be the volume of one small sphere. A sphere's volume is π<em>d</em><sup>3</sup>/6, so the larger sphere has volume 8<em>V</em>. The eight small spheres together also occupy 8<em>V</em>.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Hypothetical particle population fractions by number and volume">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Count</th><th>Group volume</th><th>Fraction by number</th><th>Fraction by volume</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Small: diameter d</th><td>8</td><td>8V</td><td>8/9 ≈ 88.9%</td><td>8/16 = 50%</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Large: diameter 2d</th><td>1</td><td>8V</td><td>1/9 ≈ 11.1%</td><td>8/16 = 50%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Nothing has been added, removed or cleaned between the two calculations. Only the weighting changes. The model has no real size unit and does not represent dust sampled from a production line.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="measurement-method">
        <h2>Keep the measurement method with the result</h2>
        <p>In its laser-diffraction discussion, <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication960-1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST's Particle Size Characterization guide, pages 110–111</a>, describes volume-based output and number or surface-area representations derived from it. It also warns that departures from spherical shape can introduce error or bias. This is a method-specific discussion, not a statement that every particle instrument starts with volume.</p>
        <p>Our exact sphere arithmetic is therefore not a conversion recipe for an irregular-dust report. Ask the laboratory which basis was measured or derived and which assumptions the reported conversion uses.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="proposal-comparison">
        <h2>Resolve the comparison before reviewing a cleaner</h2>
        <p>Return to the invented summaries. Label the first “small group: approximately 89% by number” and the second “small group: 50% by volume.” Both now describe our model correctly. Neither is a before-and-after removal percentage.</p>
        <p>For a real proposal, request the size interval, reporting basis and method beside each percentage. Have the trial team identify which result addresses the agreed cleaning requirement. Do not rank two cleaners from unlike population fractions or silently rename a volume fraction as a particle-count fraction.</p>
        <p>For the separate questions of inspected surface area and image-size descriptors, see <a href="/blog/surface-particle-inspection-report">reading a surface particle inspection report</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Bring the particle report to your cleaning review</h2>
        <p>Share the material, process stage and original report headings when discussing a dry-cleaning trial. Our team can review the application and the information needed for equipment selection. This example does not establish removal performance for a Shidike cleaner.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/cleaning-machine">Explore Cleaning Machines &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Particle%20Size%20Report%20Review">Discuss your cleaning application &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
